    "diversity_cost_finding": "Within the tested grid (symprec in [0.01,0.3], cell_bin in [0.02,0.3]) and this generator's chemical diversity (985/992 distinct reduced formulas), coarsening cell_bin from 0.02 to 0.30 at fixed symprec lowered p from ~0.75 to ~0.50-0.52 with NO measurable loss of fingerprint distinctness at n~400-992 (n_distinct stayed within 1-4 of n at every setting; n_eff stayed within rounding of n). The apparent stability-diversity trade-off did not bite at this population size/diversity: collisions are rare regardless of cell_bin because composition+orbit information alone already separates almost all pairs. A trade-off would be expected to emerge at larger n or with a known low-diversity generator (the release flags WyLLM-vanilla as having only 441/1000 distinct compositions), which was out of scope for this sweep's arm-pair requirement (WyLLM-vanilla has no raw/DFT paired subset in this corpus)."
  },
  "verdict": {
    "measured_p_range": {
      "min_p": 0.6336633663366337,
      "max_p": 0.7864077669902912
    },
    "flag_back_trigger_tripped": false,
    "statement": "Measured flip rate p ranges from 0.634 to 0.786 across all valid family/arm combinations (pooled Arm A p=0.762, pooled Arm B p=0.755). The p > 0.8 flag-back trigger was NOT tripped at any setting or family (all point estimates and their 95% CIs sit below 0.8; only two small-n families, WyCryst Arm A n=103 p=0.786 and WyFormer-letters Arm A n=104 p=0.740, are not statistically distinguishable from 0.8 after Holm-Bonferroni correction). At gamma=0.5, m=8, the pooled flip rates imply an audit needs approximately 313 (Arm B) to 335 (Arm A) fingerprints per detection -- an order of magnitude above the flip-rate-free case, but not the total-infeasibility regime that a p>0.8 result would have implied.",
    "caveat_MatterGen_indexing": "The release's CHGNet-relaxed MatterGen file (source 65498775.csv.gz) carries a re-sequenced 'material_id' column (0..9999, NOT the generation index); naively joining it to the raw/DFT files (which key on 'gen_N.cif') on that field gives a formula-match rate of 0.01% -- a false pairing that would have produced a meaningless flip rate near-certainly close to 1.0 for random unrelated pairs. The true generation index is preserved in that file's 'id' column; joining on it recovers a 100% formula-match rate. This is a data-indexing quirk of the released CHGNet_fix file, not evidence about the WyFormer-DiffCSP++10k branch specifically (the corpus's WyFormer-DiffCSP++10k key was not one of the six specified Arm A families, and was not tested here since no paired DFT/raw subset for it exists in this corpus)."
  },
